  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM MDT Saturday SEPTEMBER 6 2026\n\nGoing into Tuesday evening and overnight a few storms may be \npossible in the southern most portions of the area. Low temperatures \nare progged to be in the mid 40s west of I-25 and in the 50s east. \nWith mostly clear skies we could see a few high elevation spots get \ninto the high 30s. A zonal flow west flow aloft on the north edge of \na ridge will keep things mostly dry. Wednesday afternoon humidity \nvalues drop into the teens for our northern and western fire zones. \nThursday looks even drier with single digit humidity values with \npoor overnight recoveries. If a shortwave is timed to pass during \nany of these day's afternoon hours, enhanced 700 mbar flow could mix \ndown to the surface increasing wind gust potential. Therefore, \nelevated to critical fire weather conditions are likely to manifest \nduring this time frame through Friday evening. Model ensemble \nprobabilities for below 25 percent RH and wind gusts above 25 mph \nare between 70-100 percent generally along and west of I-25 at this \ntimeframe, which is rather aggressive from that guidance. There is a \nchance that Friday could feature a little more atmospheric moisture \nfrom Hurricane Marie as upper level moisture from it is advected \nwell east over the intermountain regions. That would be the better \nof chances to see showers and storms during this stretch.\n\nSaturday could be the soonest we see improvements to the dry \nconditions. An upper level trough is being hinted at in global \nmodels to approach the Pacific Coast. The timing and magnitude of \nthis trough is largely unknown with high variance in each model and \nfrom run to run. The shutoff of the monsoonal flow thanks to the \nplacement of the upper level ridge to the south could make getting \nmoisture north ahead of this possible system a challenge. Being so \nfar in the future that detail will resolve as we get closer.",
